  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 310 AM Tuesday...\n \nKEY MESSAGE 1... Another hot and humid day today, with isolated\nto scattered afternoon showers/thunderstorms with strong to \nsevere wind gusts possible.\n\nSurface high pressure has started retrograding back towards the \nsoutheast coast, allowing for surface winds over central NC to back \nto be more westerly. Aloft, northwesterly flow around the top of the \nridge will allow for a few disturbances to track towards and \npotentially through the region today from the Ohio River Valley. The \nfirst system is expected to weaken as it passes over the mountains, \nwith only slightly increased cloud cover expected in the north this \nmorning. Another disturbance looks to move across the region this \nafternoon into this evening, bringing a risk of isolated to \nscattered thunderstorms. Model guidance continues to differ on how \nmuch convection will be able to persist across central NC. However, \nslightly stronger shear today should support stronger and better \norganized storms than in previous days. As a result, a few storms \ncould produce strong to damaging wind gusts, particularly where \nsufficient instability develops. On the other hand, if increased \nmorning cloud cover persists further south through the day than \ncurrently forecast, this could limit the amount of instability \navailable for storms and could ultimately lead to a decrease in \nstorm coverage and severity. Thus, isolated to scattered strong to \nsevere storms remain possible this afternoon, with damaging wind \ngusts as the main concern.\n\nIn terms of temperatures, highs this afternoon should reach the \nlower 90s in the north to the upper 90s in the south. Combined with \nhumidity, heat indices should peak in the mid 90s north to the \nlow/mid 100s in the south. Thus, a Heat Advisory has been issued for \nSampson County where the heat index is forecast to reach 105 \ndegrees. Elsewhere, Moderate to Major HeatRisk indicates that heat \nillnesses are still possible for those without adequate cooling and \nhydration today, especially those sensitive to high heat. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Summertime heat and humidity will continue this week, though \nprobably lessened/interrupted by a series of MCSs/MCVs and related \noutflow and debris cloudiness that will track across the OH Valley \nand srn Middle Atlantic with accompanying strong-severe wind \npotential.\n\nAround a sub-tropical ridge that will retrograde from the Gulf to \nthe Mid-South through the weekend, cntl NC will be influenced by a \nseries of (mostly convectively-enhanced) mid-level disturbances in \nwnwly flow aloft from the Midwest to the Middle Atlantic.\n\nAt the surface, a convectively-modulated frontal zone will waver \nfrom the central Plains to the srn Middle Atlantic through most of \nthe week, then edge swd across NC late Fri-Sat. An onshore/ely flow \ncomponent in post-frontal high pressure over the Middle Atlantic \nwill favor less hot temperatures in cntl NC this weekend, when the \ncenter of the heat should retreat swwd and into the TN Valley and \nMid-South. \n\nWhile the prevailing wnwly flow pattern aloft will be favorable for \nMCS development and maintenance around the sub-tropical ridge, \nmesoscale uncertainties with their timing, track, and maintenance \nand/or redevelopment east of the Appalachians will have implications \non precipitation and wind potential into cntl NC, along with \npotential lessening of what will otherwise be a hot and seasonably \nhumid stretch this week. Pending those mesoscale influences, the \nairmass will otherwise be supportive of temperatures well into the \n90s, with heat index values in the 100-105F or so range over all but \nthe nw Piedmont.",
